Falsifies `system_tests::user::tests::an_elf_that_asks_to_be_loaded_over_the_kernel_is_refused`.

Architecture: aarch64, riscv64, x86_64

The defect is the removal of the half check in `paging::Mapper::map`, which is the guard the
test's own doc comment says it is testing: the refusal is "by construction, not by a check we
remembered to write", because the user `Mapper` is built with `Half::Low` and a high address
is not a thing it can express. This patch makes it expressible.

`WrongHalf` is the only thing between a hostile ELF naming `KERNEL_VA_BASE` as its load
address and a walk down the low tables with the top bits thrown away. With the guard gone the
walk proceeds: the hardware selects the table set from bits the walk never sees, so the
mapping is built somewhere nobody will consult, and the loader returns success.

Expected to fail: `assert_eq!(load(&image).err(), Some(LoadError::Unmappable(
MapError::WrongHalf)), "the kernel agreed to map a user program on top of itself")`.

**The `map_range` guard at line 636 is left alone on purpose.** `Mapper` checks the half in
two places, `map` and `unmap`, and a patch that removed both would be a larger edit proving no
more. The loader reaches this through `map`.

**This is the clean case for the §31 shape**: the assertion that fires is the assertion the
claim is stated in, with nothing upstream. A load that succeeds returns `Ok`, `err()` is
`None`, and the equality is the first thing that looks.

Replayed on all three ISAs. The hunk is portable code, so one patch serves every leg. On 2026-10-03 (UTC), `cargo xtask test --arch riscv64 --test an_elf_that_asks_to_be_loaded_over_the_kernel_is_refused` and the same with `--arch x86_64` both came back red at `system_tests/src/user/tests.rs:415`, the assertion this record predicts, which is where aarch64 was already red.

diff --git a/crates/paging/src/lib.rs b/crates/paging/src/lib.rs
index 903a72a4..de4c383c 100644
--- a/crates/paging/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/paging/src/lib.rs
@@ -561,10 +561,6 @@ where
     pub fn map(&mut self, va: u64, pa: u64, flags: Flags) -> Result<(), MapError> {
         // The hardware selects the table set from the top bits before it touches an index, so
         // mapping a high address into the low tables would build a mapping the CPU never consults.
-        if !F::is_in_half(self.half, va) {
-            return Err(MapError::WrongHalf);
-        }
-
         if !va.is_multiple_of(PAGE_SIZE) || !pa.is_multiple_of(PAGE_SIZE) {
             return Err(MapError::Misaligned);
         }
